add(("title", "Finite Element Analysis of Modified Green–Lindsay Thermoelasticity for Functionally Graded and Porous Media Under a Line Heat Source: A Galerkin State-Space Formulation"))
add(("authors", "[Author names, affiliations and corresponding-author e-mail to be inserted]"))
add(("h1u", "Abstract"))
V = R["valid"]; CV = R["conv"]
add(("abstract",
 "The modified Green–Lindsay (MGL) theory of thermoelasticity removes the displacement discontinuity of the original Green–Lindsay model by adding a strain-rate term to the constitutive law. "
 "Closed-form solutions of MGL line-source problems exist only for homogeneous media. This paper develops a Galerkin finite element solution of the MGL equations for an unbounded medium whose properties vary radially, either through a functionally graded (FG) composition or through porosity, and which is heated by a line heat source. "
 "The line source is regularised as a compact heat-generating disc, the operators $(1+\\tau_1\\partial_t)$ and $(1+\\tau_0\\partial_t)$ are removed by two auxiliary state variables, and the resulting first-order system is marched with the trapezoidal rule, which reduces to a Newmark average-acceleration and Crank–Nicolson pair. "
 "A second route, a Laplace-domain finite element solution inverted numerically, is used as an independent check of the time integration. "
 "The scheme is validated against an exact modified Bessel function solution that is derived here for uniform media with arbitrary property ratios. For the homogeneous copper data of the base problem, the finite element temperature history agrees with the exact one to " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent, displacement to " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][1], 2) + " per cent and stress to " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][2], 2) + " to " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][3], 2) + " per cent in the relative L2 sense, and the Laplace-domain and time-domain finite element solutions agree to better than 0.06 per cent in temperature. "
 "Quadratic elements reduce the temperature error by more than two orders of magnitude between h = 0.05 and h = 0.0125, and the dominant remaining error is the source regularisation, which falls as the square of the source radius for the displacement and more slowly for the stresses. "
 "The studies show that grading the properties from a low-conductivity, stiff core to a copper-like far field delays the thermal front at r = 0.3 from t = " + fx(arrival("H0"), 4) + " to " + fx(arrival("FG_n1"), 3) + " for a linear grading, raises the temperature behind the front, and raises the peak radial stress by a factor of " + fx(abs(peak_abs("FG_n1", 0, 2)[0] / peak_abs("H0", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + ". "
 "Uniform porosity of 0.3 lowers the local wave speed by " + fx(100 * (1 - R["prof"]["p0.3"]["c"][0] / R["prof"]["hom"]["c"][0]), 1) + " per cent and raises the temperature at the observation point by " + fx(100 * (at("P_0.3", 0, 0, 0.3) / at("H0", 0, 0, 0.3) - 1), 0) + " per cent. "
 "Without the strain-rate term an elastic wavefront with a stress jump appears and is smeared by the mesh, whereas the MGL term removes it. The sharp stress peak at the thermal front is under-resolved by about a factor of two, and this and other limits are reported."))
add(("kw", "modified Green–Lindsay thermoelasticity; finite element method; Galerkin; functionally graded material; porous material; line heat source; state-space time integration"))

H1("1. Introduction")
P("Classical coupled thermoelasticity assumes Fourier conduction and therefore predicts an infinite speed for thermal disturbances. For heating on very short time scales, for example by laser or particle beams, or in nanoscale components, that prediction is not acceptable, and a family of generalized theories has been proposed in which one or more relaxation times appear in the energy equation or in the constitutive law. "
  "The Lord–Shulman theory adds one relaxation time to the heat flux law, the Green–Lindsay theory adds two relaxation times to the constitutive and energy equations, and the Green–Naghdi theories introduce a thermal displacement. Reviews of these theories and of their modifications are given in [9]. "
  "The original Green–Lindsay model has a well known defect: the displacement can be discontinuous at the wavefront. Yu, Xue and Tian removed this defect by adding a strain-rate term with the same relaxation time that multiplies the temperature rate, and Quintanilla analysed the qualitative properties of the resulting modified Green–Lindsay (MGL) system. "
  "Later contributions established fundamental solutions [3], a Galerkin-type representation [4], infinite-speed behaviour [2] and applications to photothermal problems in semiconductors [6].")
P("The reference problem for the present work is the line heat source in an unbounded isotropic homogeneous MGL medium solved by Singh and Mukhopadhyay [1]. Using a Laplace transform and a displacement potential, they found that the homogeneous MGL solution contains one wave of finite speed together with a diffusive part, that the displacement is continuous, and that the discontinuity is confined to the hoop stress. "
  "This differs from the line source solutions of the Moore–Gibson–Thompson theory, which show two wavefronts [7, 8]. All of these solutions rely on the Laplace transform, and that route needs property data that are constant in space, so that the transformed equation reduces to a product of two Helmholtz operators with Bessel function solutions.")
P("Practical heat-affected media are rarely homogeneous. Functionally graded materials replace the sharp interface between a metal and a ceramic by a smooth variation of composition, and a large literature treats their thermal and thermoelastic response, including transient cooling shocks of graded plates [11, 12]. "
  "Porosity changes stiffness, conductivity and density, either as a by-product of manufacture or deliberately, and the design of thermoelastic composites with maximum stiffness and heat transfer by topology optimisation is a related field in which finite element analysis is the working tool [10]. "
  "For the MGL theory with graded properties, Shivay and Mukhopadhyay developed a complete Galerkin-type finite element approach for a functionally graded hollow disk [5]. "
  "To the knowledge of the authors, which rests on the reference list of [1] and not on a systematic survey, the line heat source in a radially graded or porous MGL medium has not been treated before; the list of references is intentionally limited to sources whose details could be verified (see the note after the reference list).")
P("The contribution of this paper is a complete and verified finite element formulation for that problem. It has four elements. First, the governing equations are written for spatially variable property ratios in a form that keeps the divergence structure, so that no derivative of a material property is needed. Second, the line source is regularised by a compact disc whose radius is a controlled parameter, and the regularisation error is measured, not assumed. Third, the operators $(1+\\tau_1\\partial_t)$ and $(1+\\tau_0\\partial_t)$ are eliminated with two auxiliary state variables, so that a standard unconditionally stable one-step method can be used. Fourth, the scheme is verified against an exact solution that is derived for uniform media with arbitrary property ratios, which makes it possible to test the porosity model rigorously, and against an independent Laplace-domain finite element solution, which makes it possible to test the graded case.")
P("The paper is organised as follows. Section 2 states the governing equations. Section 3 defines the grading and porosity models and the local wave speed. Section 4 derives the exact reference solution for uniform media. Section 5 gives the weak form and the finite element matrices, Section 6 the time integration and the Laplace-domain alternative, and Section 7 the verification measures. Section 8 presents the results, Section 9 discusses limitations and Section 10 concludes.")

FIG("Figure_1.png", "Schematic of the problem. (a) Geometry with the graded zone and the far field. (b) Finite element mesh with the aligned source disc and the truncation boundary. (c) Solution procedure. This figure is a schematic and contains no computed data.")
H1("2. Governing equations")
H2("2.1 MGL thermoelasticity")
P("Let $u_i$ be the displacement, $\\theta$ the temperature increment above the reference temperature $T_0$, $e$ the dilatation and $\\epsilon_{ij}$ the strain. In the MGL theory the stress and the equation of motion read")
EQ(r"\sigma_{ij}=\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)\left[\lambda e\delta_{ij}+2\mu\epsilon_{ij}-\gamma\theta\delta_{ij}\right],\qquad \rho\,\partial_t^2 u_i=\sigma_{ij,j}", "e1")
P("where $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lamé moduli, $\\rho$ the density, $\\gamma=(3\\lambda+2\\mu)\\alpha_t$ the thermomechanical coupling coefficient, $\\alpha_t$ the coefficient of linear thermal expansion and $\\tau_1$ the relaxation time that multiplies both the strain rate and the temperature rate. The energy equation and the heat flux law are")
EQ(r"\rho c_E\,\partial_t\theta+\gamma T_0\,\partial_t e=-\nabla\cdot q+Q,\qquad \left(1+\tau_0\partial_t\right)q=-k\nabla\theta", "e2")
P("with $c_E$ the specific heat at constant strain, $k$ the thermal conductivity, $q$ the heat flux, $Q$ the volumetric heat source and $\\tau_0$ the relaxation time of the heat flux. The sign convention follows [1]: the source $Q$ enters the energy balance after the operator $(1+\\tau_0\\partial_t)$ has been applied, so that Eqs. (@e1@) and (@e2@) lead to a heat equation in which $Q$ is not differentiated in time. This choice is needed to reproduce the reference solution and it is kept throughout.")
H2("2.2 Nondimensional radial equations")
P("The nondimensionalisation of the base problem is used with the reference (copper) data of Table 1, namely")
EQ(r"r'=\eta c_0 r,\quad t'=\eta c_0^2 t,\quad u'=\eta c_0 u,\quad \theta'=\frac{\theta}{T_0},\quad \sigma'=\frac{\sigma}{\lambda_0+2\mu_0},\qquad c_0=\sqrt{\frac{\lambda_0+2\mu_0}{\rho_0}},\ \eta=\frac{\rho_0 c_{E0}}{k_0}", "scale")
P("With the subscript $0$ denoting the reference values, the dimensionless constants are")
EQ(r"\delta_1=\frac{\gamma_0 T_0}{\lambda_0+2\mu_0},\quad \delta_2=\frac{\gamma_0}{\rho_0 c_{E0}},\quad \lambda_1=\frac{\lambda_0}{\lambda_0+2\mu_0},\quad \kappa=1-\lambda_1,\quad \epsilon=\delta_1\delta_2")
TAB(*T1())
P("For copper the values are $\\delta_1=" + g(CONST["d1"], 5) + "$, $\\delta_2=" + g(CONST["d2"], 5) + "$, $\\epsilon=" + g(CONST["eps"], 4) + "$ and $\\lambda_1=" + g(CONST["lambda1"], 5) + "$. The length and time scales are $1/(\\eta c_0)=" + g(1 / (CONST["eta"] * CONST["v"]) * 1e9, 3) + "$ nm and $1/(\\eta c_0^2)=" + g(1 / (CONST["eta"] * CONST["v"] ** 2) * 1e12, 3) + "$ ps, which is a first warning that the dimensionless numbers below correspond to nanoscale lengths and picosecond times for copper, a point taken up in Section 9. "
  "Properties that vary with the radial coordinate are described by dimensionless ratios $\\hat\\lambda(r)$, $\\hat\\mu(r)$, $\\hat k(r)$, $\\hat\\rho(r)$, $\\hat c(r)$ and $\\hat\\alpha(r)$ relative to the reference values, and the radial displacement $u(r,t)$ is the only displacement component. The strain components are")
EQ(r"\epsilon_{rr}=\partial_r u,\qquad \epsilon_{\phi\phi}=\frac{u}{r},\qquad e=\partial_r u+\frac{u}{r}")
P("The thermoelastic stresses before the operator $(1+\\tau_1\\partial_t)$ is applied are denoted $S_{rr}$ and $S_{\\phi\\phi}$:")
EQ(r"S_{rr}=P\,\partial_r u+L\,\frac{u}{r}-\delta_1 g\,\theta,\qquad S_{\\phi\\phi}=L\,\partial_r u+P\,\frac{u}{r}-\delta_1 g\,\theta".replace("\\\\","\\"))
P("The dimensionless coefficients $P(r)$, $L(r)$ and $g(r)$ are the P-wave modulus, the first Lamé modulus and the thermal stress coefficient relative to $\\lambda_0+2\\mu_0$ and $\\gamma_0$; they are defined in Section 3 and they equal $1$, $\\lambda_1$ and $1$ in the reference medium. The MGL stresses and the equation of motion become")
EQ(r"\sigma_{rr}=\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)S_{rr},\qquad \sigma_{\phi\phi}=\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)S_{\phi\phi}")
EQ(r"\hat\rho\,\partial_t^2 u=\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)\left[\partial_r S_{rr}+\frac{S_{rr}-S_{\phi\phi}}{r}\right]")
P("For the energy equation, Eqs. (@e1@) and (@e2@) are combined and the divergence form is retained, which is important because it means that the weak form below needs no derivative of $\\hat k$:")
EQ(r"\frac{1}{r}\partial_r\left(r\hat k\,\partial_r\theta\right)=\left(1+\tau_0\partial_t\right)\left[\hat\rho\hat c\,\partial_t\theta+\delta_2 g\,\partial_t e\right]-Q", "heat")
P("Here $\\hat\\rho\\hat c$ is the product of the density and specific heat ratios. A homogeneous medium with unit ratios gives back the equations of [1].")
H2("2.3 Source, initial and far-field conditions")
P("The line source of strength $Q_0$ per unit length, switched on at $t=0$, is")
EQ(r"Q(r,t)=\frac{Q_0}{2\pi r}\,\delta(r)\,H(t)", "src")
P("and the initial state is quiescent:")
EQ(r"u=\partial_t u=\theta=\partial_t\theta=0\quad\text{at}\quad t=0")
P("The medium is unbounded. For the finite element model the domain is truncated at $r=R_{out}$ and a Dirichlet far-field condition is applied,")
EQ(r"u(0,t)=0,\qquad \partial_r\theta(0,t)=0,\qquad u(R_{out},t)=\theta(R_{out},t)=0", "bc")
P("The truncation is justified by the finite speed of the MGL wave: no disturbance can reach $R_{out}$ before $t=R_{out}/c_{max}$, with $c_{max}$ the largest local wave speed defined in Eq. (@wave@) below, so that the Dirichlet condition has no influence as long as the computation stops before that time. Beyond that time the condition would reflect waves. "
  "This is a statement about the hyperbolic part of the solution; the diffusive part has no finite front, but its amplitude at $R_{out}$ is below machine precision for the computations reported here. The check in Section 8.2 confirms this: with $R_{out}=1.6$ and $R_{out}=4.5$ the temperature history at $r=0.3$ is identical to all printed digits, whereas with $R_{out}=1.0$, for which the front arrives at $t\\approx" + fx(1.0 / 7.012, 3) + "$, the history is visibly changed. No absorbing boundary condition is used.")
P("An exact global balance follows from integrating Eq. (@heat@) over the domain, provided that no flux leaves it. With $H(t)$ the heat content,")
EQ(r"\mathcal{H}(t)=\int_0^{R_{out}}\left(\hat\rho\hat c\,\theta+\delta_2 g\,e\right)r\,dr,\qquad \mathcal{H}+\tau_0\,\partial_t\mathcal{H}=\frac{Q_0}{2\pi}\,t\quad\Rightarrow\quad \mathcal{H}(t)=\frac{Q_0}{2\pi}\left[t-\tau_0\left(1-e^{-t/\tau_0}\right)\right]", "heatc")
P("This identity is independent of the property distribution and of $\\tau_1$. It is therefore a strong test of the discrete energy balance in graded media, and it is used as such in Section 8.2.")
